<Ui xmlns="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/ui/"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.blizzard.com/wow/FrameXML/UI.xsd">

	<Script file="ExportUI.lua" />

	<!--
		One action. Checkbox, icon, name, key.

		The row is the **unit of choice** - layers are groups and keys are boundaries, but what
		the user actually ticks is an action (`devdocs/building-export-import.md`). So the whole row is a
		button and clicking anywhere on it toggles; the checkbox is there to be read, not aimed at.

		Art matches the main list and the spell picker (`ClickCastList-ButtonBackground`). Three
		lists in one addon that look like three different things is three things to learn.
	-->
	<Button name="DebindExportRowTemplate" mixin="DebindExportRowMixin" virtual="true">
		<Size x="400" y="28"/>
		<Layers>
			<Layer level="BACKGROUND">
				<Texture parentKey="Background" atlas="ClickCastList-ButtonBackground">
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" x="-7" y="0"/>
						<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="7" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</Texture>
				<!--
					The key boundary. Groups are drawn as a **line**, not a header row - the same
					choice the main list made, and for the same reason: a header would promise
					that what follows fires in the order shown, and this window shows one layer
					at a time so it has no standing to say that.
				-->
				<Texture parentKey="GroupBorder" atlas="Options_HorizontalDivider" hidden="true">
					<Size y="1"/>
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" relativePoint="TOPLEFT" x="-4" y="0"/>
						<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" relativePoint="TOPRIGHT" x="4" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</Texture>
			</Layer>
			<Layer level="ARTWORK">
				<!-- Level with the header's checkbox, which starts at the same inset. -->
				<Texture parentKey="Icon">
					<Size x="22" y="22"/>
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="LEFT" x="5" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</Texture>
				<!--
					The key sits on every row even though a group shares one. Reading down a
					column is how you find the key you meant to send; making only the first row
					of a group carry it means the answer moves when the sort changes.

					**Declared before the name, because the name hangs off it.** A `relativeKey`
					pointing at a sibling that does not exist yet does not take, and a FontString
					that ends up with one of its two horizontal points missing has no width and
					draws nothing at all.
				-->
				<FontString parentKey="Key" inherits="GameFontDisableSmall" maxLines="1" justifyH="RIGHT">
					<Size x="120" y="0"/>
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="RIGHT" x="-6" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</FontString>
				<FontString parentKey="Name" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" maxLines="1" justifyH="LEFT">
					<Anchors>
						<Anchor point="LEFT" relativeKey="$parent.Icon" relativePoint="RIGHT" x="6" y="0"/>
						<Anchor point="RIGHT" relativeKey="$parent.Key" relativePoint="LEFT" x="-6" y="0"/>
					</Anchors>
				</FontString>
			</Layer>
			<!--
				**A picked row is lit, not ticked.** Same art and same layer the main window uses on
				its selected rows, so "this one is picked" looks one way across the addon.

				It also settles which rung is which. A checkbox on every row put three columns of
				boxes down the list and the eye could not tell the layer's box from an action's; a
				lit row cannot be mistaken for the header above it.
			-->
			<Layer level="OVERLAY">
				<Texture parentKey="SelectedHighlight" atlas="auctionhouse-ui-row-select" alpha=".8" hidden="true" alphaMode="ADD"/>
			</Layer>
			<Layer level="HIGHLIGHT">
				<Texture parentKey="FrameHighlight" atlas="ClickCastList-ButtonHighlight" setAllPoints="true" alphaMode="ADD"/>
			</Layer>
		</Layers>
		<Scripts>
			<OnClick method="OnClick"/>
			<OnEnter method="OnEnter"/>
			<OnLeave method="OnLeave"/>
		</Scripts>
	</Button>

	<!--
		A layer. The window's outer axis, because it is the only grouping actually stored: a key
		group crosses layers and so has nowhere to live (`devdocs/building-export-import.md`).

		Its checkbox is the middle rung of the three: all -> layer -> action.
	-->
	<Button name="DebindExportLayerTemplate" inherits="ListHeaderThreeSliceTemplate" mixin="DebindExportLayerMixin" virtual="true">
		<Size x="400" y="26"/>
		<Frames>
			<!--
				**This checkbox takes its own clicks**, unlike the one on a row. Here the two
				gestures mean different things - the bar collapses, the box selects - so the box has
				to be able to say "not me" by swallowing the click itself.

				The header text is pushed clear of it in `OnLoad` rather than re-anchored here.
				`ListHeaderThreeSliceMixin` owns that anchor and offers `AdjustTextOffset` for
				exactly this, and writing a second anchor would leave two rules for one position.
			-->
			<CheckButton parentKey="Check" inherits="MinimalCheckboxArtTemplate">
				<Size x="24" y="24"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="LEFT" x="5" y="0"/>
				</Anchors>
			</CheckButton>
		</Frames>
		<Scripts>
			<OnLoad method="OnLoad"/>
			<OnClick method="OnClick"/>
			<OnEnter method="OnEnter"/>
			<OnLeave method="OnLeave"/>
		</Scripts>
	</Button>

	<!--
		**A panel, not a window.** It is one of the three the main frame swaps between, reached by
		the Export tab (`PANELS` in `DebindUI.lua`). So it carries no chrome of its own: no portrait,
		no title, no drag, no `UISpecialFrames` registration. Those belong to the frame that hosts it.

		**A child of that frame, declared from another file.** `parent=` plus `parentKey=` seats it
		exactly where `OverviewPanel` sits, which is what lets `PANELS` name it by key. It could not
		be one while this file belonged to the load-on-demand addon: at the moment `DebindUI.xml` was
		read none of it existed, so it stood parented to `UIParent` and was reparented on the first
		press. That whole road is gone with the addon boundary (`devdocs/building-export-import.md`).
		The load-on-demand half that remains is the **data**, not the panel.

		The offsets below are Overview's, taken from `DebindUI.xml`. They used to be the ones this
		panel picked while it was its own window - which kept working, because the frame it lands in
		is a `PortraitFrameTemplate` too and the title bar being cleared is the same height. Working
		is not the same as landing on the line the tab next door lands on.

		No keyboard on the frame. The output dialog takes focus when there is something to copy and
		ESC there only drops focus, so nothing here touches `SetPropagateKeyboardInput`, which
		cannot be called in combat anyway.

		**No `enableMouse`, and that is a change from being a window.** A window wants to swallow
		clicks that land on it; a panel covering the whole frame that swallows them takes the title
		bar with it, and the window stops being draggable from any tab but Overview. The interactive
		children catch their own clicks and the rest belong to the frame underneath, which is how
		`OverviewPanel` and `MissingPanel` already sit.

		**`useParentLevel` for the same kind of reason.** The list inside pins itself to level 3, a
		number chosen when this was its own frame at the bottom of its own stack. Letting the panel
		take a level of its own would put that 3 underneath it and the list would draw behind its
		own inset.

		**`preferredWidth` is the one number this panel asks the frame for**, and it is the only size
		written here: pinned to the host on all four sides, a `<Size>` of its own would never be the
		rectangle it ends up with. It used to be read back out of `GetWidth()` in `OnLoad` precisely
		so there would not be two numbers - which only worked while the panel stood unanchored at
		load, and would now answer with the host's width, this value fed back.
	-->
	<Frame name="DebindExportPanel" parent="DebindFrame" parentKey="ExportPanel" hidden="true"
		useParentLevel="true" setAllPoints="true" mixin="DebindExportPanelMixin">
		<KeyValues>
			<KeyValue key="preferredWidth" value="470" type="number"/>
		</KeyValues>
		<Frames>
			<Frame parentKey="ScrollBoxBackground" useParentLevel="true" inherits="TooltipBackdropTemplate">
				<!--
					The same combination Blizzard's click binding window puts under its list, and the
					same one Overview uses (`DebindUI.xml`): a thin border over black instead of
					`InsetFrameTemplate`'s old bevel, so the box belongs to the same age as the row
					art laid on top of it (`ClickCastList-ButtonBackground`). This panel took that art
					from Overview and left the container behind.

					**The rectangle is Overview's too** - left 4, top -85, right -31. These are one
					window's three tabs, and a box that starts on a different line in each makes the
					list jump as you switch.

					**The bottom is 50, which is the click binding window's** - the band it leaves for
					the button row on the frame's bottom edge. Overview's 10 is the band for its layer
					tabs, and this panel has no such row.
				-->
				<KeyValues>
					<KeyValue key="backdropBorderColor" value="DARKGRAY_COLOR" type="global"/>
					<KeyValue key="backdropColor" value="BLACK_FONT_COLOR" type="global"/>
				</KeyValues>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" x="4" y="-85"/>
					<Anchor point="TOPRIGHT" x="-31" y="-85"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" x="4" y="50"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="-31" y="50"/>
				</Anchors>
			</Frame>

			<!--
				The top rung: everything.

				Its click is attached in `OnLoad`, not with `method=` here. `method=` resolves
				against the **element's own** mixin, and a plain Blizzard checkbox template has none,
				so naming the frame's method reaches nothing. The three chrome widgets below are
				wired the same way for the same reason.

				**Declared after the inset, because it hangs off it.** An anchor pointing at a sibling
				that does not exist yet is dropped without a word (`tools/check-xml-anchors.js`).
			-->
			<CheckButton parentKey="SelectAllCheck" inherits="MinimalCheckboxArtTemplate">
				<Size x="24" y="24"/>
				<!--
					Hung off the inset rather than off the frame, so it keeps one left edge with the list
					wherever the rectangle goes.
				-->
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="TOPLEFT" x="4" y="4"/>
				</Anchors>
				<Layers>
					<Layer level="ARTWORK">
						<FontString parentKey="Text" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" justifyH="LEFT">
							<Anchors>
								<Anchor point="LEFT" relativePoint="RIGHT" x="2" y="0"/>
							</Anchors>
						</FontString>
					</Layer>
				</Layers>
			</CheckButton>

			<Button parentKey="ScrollBox" inherits="WowScrollBoxList" frameLevel="3">
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="TOPLEFT" x="5" y="-5"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="-5" y="5"/>
				</Anchors>
				<Layers>
					<Layer level="ARTWORK">
						<FontString parentKey="EmptyText" hidden="true" inherits="GameFontDisable" justifyH="CENTER">
							<Size x="340" y="0"/>
							<Anchors>
								<Anchor point="TOP" x="0" y="-40"/>
							</Anchors>
						</FontString>
					</Layer>
				</Layers>
			</Button>
			<EventFrame parentKey="ScrollBar" inherits="MinimalScrollBar">
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="TOPRIGHT" x="5" y="-5"/>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ScrollBoxBackground" relativePoint="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="5" y="5"/>
				</Anchors>
			</EventFrame>

			<!--
				**The row under the list stands on the frame's bottom edge, not under the inset.**
				That is where the click binding window puts its own
				(`Blizzard_ClickBindingUI.xml`: `BOTTOMRIGHT -6, 5`, height 23), and the band between
				the list and that row is left empty on purpose. Hanging this off the bottom of the
				inset instead - which is what this panel did - floats it in the middle of that band
				with dead space underneath.
			-->
			<Button parentKey="GenerateButton" inherits="UIPanelButtonTemplate">
				<Size x="140" y="23"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT" x="-6" y="5"/>
				</Anchors>
			</Button>
		</Frames>
		<Scripts>
			<OnLoad method="OnLoad"/>
			<OnShow method="OnShow"/>
			<OnHide method="OnHide"/>
		</Scripts>
	</Frame>

	<!--
		Where the finished string is handed over.

		**A dialog rather than a box in the window.** The string is read once, selected, copied and
		never looked at again, so a permanent panel spends height on something that is empty almost
		all of the time - and that height is exactly what the list was short of.

		**Same chrome as the other dialogs** - `DebindDialogTemplate`, which took its shape from the
		game's own import dialog (`Blizzard_ClassTalentLoadoutImportDialog.xml`). This and the paste
		dialog are the same gesture in opposite directions and a reader meets them a minute apart,
		which is why none of the geometry is written out here any more.

		**No caption over the box, unlike over there.** That dialog has two boxes and has to say
		which is which; this one has a single box the title already names, and a caption repeating
		the title is a line that says nothing.

		**The close button is not decoration here.** With the X gone, it and ESC are the only ways
		out - and this is the one dialog that is deliberately left standing when its tab goes away.
	-->
	<Frame name="DebindCopyFrame" inherits="DebindDialogTemplate" mixin="DebindCopyFrameMixin">
		<Size x="460" y="185"/>
		<Frames>

			<ScrollFrame parentKey="Output" inherits="InputScrollFrameTemplate">
				<KeyValues>
					<!-- Nobody types here; a character counter over a generated string is noise. -->
					<KeyValue key="hideCharCount" value="true" type="boolean"/>
				</KeyValues>
				<Size y="80"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeKey="$parent.ContentArea" relativePoint="TOPLEFT"/>
					<Anchor point="TOPRIGHT" relativeKey="$parent.ContentArea" relativePoint="TOPRIGHT"/>
				</Anchors>
			</ScrollFrame>

			<!--
				Centred rather than paired, because there is only one thing to do here: the copy is
				Ctrl-C and this button is just the way back out. `CLOSE` is the client's own, so it
				is already in every language.
			-->
			<Button parentKey="CloseDialogButton" inherits="UIPanelButtonTemplate" text="CLOSE">
				<Size x="120" y="22"/>
				<Anchors>
					<Anchor point="BOTTOM" relativeKey="$parent.ContentArea" relativePoint="BOTTOM" x="0" y="0"/>
				</Anchors>
			</Button>
		</Frames>
		<Scripts>
			<OnLoad method="OnLoad"/>
		</Scripts>
	</Frame>

</Ui>
